 ## Background
-The concept of adaptive density control, including pruning and densification, was introduced in the original 3D Gaussian Splatting paper by Kerbl et al. [2023] to manage the trade-off between representation power and computational cost. The method uses 2D positional gradients to identify areas needing more detail (densification) and removes Gaussians with low opacity to prevent floaters. Subsequent works have adapted these principles: GES uses shape-based pruning [S1], TSP extends it to temporal domains [S1], and Triangle Splatting adapts it for triangle primitives [S2]. The "Scooping" method proposes a recycling mechanism to avoid the overhead of explicit pruning and splitting [S1].
+The concept of pruning and opacity management originated with the introduction of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) by Kerbl et al. [S3, unofficial]. The original 3DGS implementation utilized a heuristic-based densification and pruning strategy driven by 2D positional gradients and opacity thresholds to manage the number of Gaussians during training. Subsequent works have adapted these principles to different primitive types (e.g., triangles in Triangle Splatting [S2]) and optimization goals (e.g., shape-based pruning in GES [S1], temporal consistency in dynamic scenes [S1], and spatial constraints [S1]). The specific mechanism of "scooping" or component recycling was introduced to address the limitations of standard pruning in maintaining representation power without unbounded growth [S1].
 
